<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leoš Janáček</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1854">1854</a>–<a href="/years/1928">1928</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leoš Janáček was a Czech composer, born at Hukvaldy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, who spent most of his career in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a>, where he founded an organ school. Drawing on Moravian folk music and the rhythms of Czech speech, he wrote operas such as Jenůfa, Káťa Kabanová and The Cunning Little Vixen, as well as the Sinfonietta and the Glagolitic Mass. International fame came only in his last decade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leos-janacek.jpg" alt="Leoš Janáček" width="320" height="341" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unkonwn · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leo%C5%A1_Jan%C3%A1%C4%8Dek_el_1914.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
